# The World That Builds Itself

## A Material History of Everything

**Current chapter outline:** fourteen chapters in three parts, with a prologue, epilogue, and methodological afterword. Chapters 1–3 establish the framework; chapters 4–8 work backward from familiar discoveries through steam engines to the possible assembly of the universe; chapters 9–14 follow human society through collective intelligence, economic self-production, cybernetics, AI as assembly, and productive deployment. All fourteen chapters have prose drafts. This outline and the chapter files use the same reading order.

## Central argument

Every system is assembled and jointly material and Platonic. Its physical realization and its formal relations determine what it can do. The book proposes that disequilibrium drives systems toward maximum rates of energy extraction and entropy production through accessible pathways. Assembly changes the constraints on those pathways, making further assemblies possible.

**Ontological foundation:** the book proposes that absolute nothingness is impossible and that uncertainty constitutes physical reality. This is ontological indeterminacy, distinct from incomplete knowledge or a statistical description. Chapter 6 examines emergence as a natural process, the premises of this philosophical account, and its limits. Neither quantum uncertainty nor the rejection of nothingness by itself derives our universe or its history.

**Extended hypothesis:** the emergence of the universe and the emergence of AI are proposed as natural processes of assembly. AI and its physical supports develop through material resources, formal organization, learning, and maintained institutions. The universe and fundamental constants are probably assembled in the book's working conjecture. These claims require mechanisms and evidence; they do not require a unique outcome across all possible histories.

**Fine-tuning extension:** progressive constraints, stationary principles, symmetry breaking, recursive maintenance, and cumulative assembly could make fine-tuning a material process. Chapter 8 asks how apparently coordinated conditions could develop together. A justified account of parameter generation could challenge an improbability argument based on independent settings. The component mechanisms motivate this proposal without already deriving the observed universe.

**Historical asymmetry and fundamental disequilibrium:** chapter 7 investigates matter predominance, weak-interaction handedness, spatial dimensions, and the temporal arrow as questions for a material history. The proposed fundamental role of disequilibrium must explain how driving differences arise. Symmetry breaking, usable gradients, and maximum entropy-production rates remain distinct claims requiring specified connections. Chapter 8 carries the question into laws and constants.

Constraints do more than exclude alternatives. They can concentrate search, reduce the cost of a transition, or direct enough existing energy and organized effort toward a barrier to make a costly first assembly viable. A successful assembly can then increase the resources and knowledge available for subsequent transitions.

The central puzzle is how this process could explain both convergent discovery and dialectical transformation. Why do different investigators approach related discoveries within an enormous possibility space? Why can preserving successful observations require replacing the categories that previously organized them? The book investigates whether these are expressions of the same physical process that produces living organization, human society, intelligence, and artificial intelligence.

**Explanatory order:** disequilibrium supplies the proposed driver; material and formal constraints shape its operation; assembly forms organizations and changes accessible pathways; autopoiesis regenerates an organization through its ongoing operations. The resulting continuity can sustain or obstruct further assembly. Specify the regenerative network and its exchanges; persistence alone establishes neither autopoiesis nor a maximum entropy-production rate. Shared institutions, tools, and knowledge are mediating mechanisms within this account.

Cybernetics supplies the account of regulation within that sequence. Hierarchical and recurrent processes can coordinate activity; self-modeling can make the system's own capacities and limits available for further regulation. Trained artificial networks extend the question of how useful internal organization is assembled. Their history also separates the success of a capability from the methodological expectations of the researchers who named its field.

For social systems, the book synthesizes Marx and Luhmann through cybernetics. Material production sustains the participants and communications that organize production; communications establish distinctions, obligations, and rules that direct subsequent material activity. Feedback connects consequences to the preservation or revision of those relations. Chapters 11 and 12 develop this jointly material and Platonic system through the reciprocal dependence of production and regulation. The synthesis belongs to the book and preserves both thinkers' distinct explanatory commitments.

**Scope:** the cross-domain maximizing claim is a hypothesis. Chapter 2 specifies the conditions under which energy throughput and entropy production can be related. Historical evidence, established physical mechanisms, and the proposed synthesis retain distinct roles. Reproductive success, profit, useful work, and human welfare require their own measures.

**Standard of demonstration:** use natural experiments and explicit causal inference to demonstrate empirical causal arguments. Historical and observational evidence can identify effects without a newly conducted experiment when the comparison and its assumptions are defensible. Each claim must specify the proposed cause, outcome, population or system, time interval, counterfactual comparison, assignment process, competing explanations, and uncertainty. Distinguish an illustration, an association, an identified effect, and a broader causal hypothesis. A historical event is not automatically a natural experiment; mathematical deductions and cosmological origin models require evidence appropriate to their claims. The [afterword](afterword) develops this standard. The adoption of a method is not a claim that the manuscript's proposed studies have been completed.

